## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and an expectation (`expect`). Report
failures by test and step number.

```mdl
test QA-1: Draw, connect, and nest a scene on a canvas
  desc: The demo path — create a canvas drawing, draw and label shapes, connect them, and drill into a frame.
  actors: human
  covers: []
  status: passing            # 2026-10-09, recorded by autocue/draw-and-nest.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - plugin: Canvas is enabled in the plugin registry (it is off by default)
    - space: the default space is open on its Home
  steps:
    - name: Create a canvas drawing
      do: Click "Add to space", choose Drawing, pick the Canvas variant and click Create.
      expect: an empty canvas opens in a new plank, with the palette on its left
    - name: Draw and label shapes
      do: |
        With the Rectangle and Ellipse tools draw "Browser", "Agent" and "Space", double-clicking each to
        type its label; then draw a Frame and name it "Storage" in the Properties panel's Label field.
      expect: four labelled nodes on the canvas, and About counts 4 nodes
      note: A double-click on a frame opens its scene rather than editing its label.
    - name: Connect the shapes
      do: With the Smart line tool drag Browser→Space, Agent→Space and Space→Storage; then move Agent.
      expect: three links that follow the shapes as they move
    - name: Open the frame and draw inside it
      do: Click the frame's open control, draw and label "Objects" and "Sync", and link them.
      expect: the canvas drills into the frame's scene and the Up control is enabled
      note: The breadcrumb names the child scene "Untitled", not the frame's label.
    - name: Zoom back out and show the frame's contents
      do: Click Up, select the Storage frame, and turn on "Show contents" in the Properties panel.
      expect: the frame draws its nested scene live inside it
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Draw a nested architecture diagram of composer-app
  desc: |
    A complex diagram across three scenes — composer-app's top level, with the plugins and the client each
    drawn as a nested scene inside a frame — every node and link taken from the packages' dependencies.
  actors: human
  covers: []
  status: passing            # 2026-10-09, recorded by autocue/composer-uml.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - plugin: Canvas is enabled in the plugin registry (it is off by default)
    - space: the default space is open on its Home
  steps:
    - name: Create a canvas drawing
      do: Click "Add to space", choose Drawing, pick the Canvas variant and click Create.
      expect: an empty canvas opens in a new plank, with the palette on its left
    - name: Draw the top level
      do: |
        Draw and label "Composer App" and "App Framework" (rectangles) and "EDGE" (ellipse); add two frames
        from the toolbar's "+" menu, name them "Plugins" and "Client" in the Properties panel's Label field,
        and move and resize each into place.
      expect: three labelled shapes and two labelled frames; About counts 5 nodes and 3 scenes
      note: |
        Frames come from the "+" menu, not the Frame tool: a frame drawn with the tool keeps the drag
        preview's child scene id, so every drawn frame shares one child scene (About stays at 2 scenes and
        each frame shows the other's contents).
    - name: Connect the top level by dependency
      do: |
        With the Smart line tool drag Composer App→Plugins, Composer App→App Framework,
        Plugins→App Framework, Plugins→Client and Client→EDGE.
      expect: five links, each attached to both of its ends
    - name: Open Plugins and draw the plugins inside it
      do: |
        Click the Plugins frame's open control; draw and label Sheet, Markdown, Table, Canvas, Illustrator
        and Space; link Sheet→Markdown, Markdown→Space, Table→Space, Canvas→Illustrator and Illustrator→Space.
      expect: the canvas drills into the frame's scene (Up enabled) and shows six nodes and five links
    - name: Open Client and draw its subsystems
      do: |
        Click Up, open the Client frame, draw and label Services, ECHO, HALO and MESH; link Services→ECHO,
        Services→HALO, Services→MESH and ECHO→MESH.
      expect: a second, separate nested scene with four nodes and four links
    - name: Go up and show both nested scenes live
      do: Click Up, then select each frame and turn on "Show contents" in the Properties panel.
      expect: each frame draws its own nested scene live inside it, alongside the top-level diagram
```

```mdl
test QA-4: Showcase clip
  desc: |
    The 20s showcase clip: Select a shape in the Composer architecture drawing, read its properties, and rename it. Recorded light at 60 fps with no ident, captions or
    count-in by `tools/autocue/showcase/overnight.mjs`; the clip is the `clip: canvas` step.
  actors: human
  status: unverified        # driven by autocue/showcase.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - plugin-canvas enabled
  steps:
    - name: Create the space from its template
      do: Open the app menu, choose Create space, pick DXOS Architecture, and open the Composer drawing.
      expect: the drawing opens
    - name: Read a shape
      do: Select the React UI box and scroll its Properties panel.
      expect: the box is selected and its properties show
    - name: Rename it
      do: Turn off read-only, double-click the box, and type React components.
      expect: the box shows its new label
```

```mdl
test QA-5: Copy a tldraw drawing as a canvas
  desc: |
    The illustrator's "Copy as Canvas" action converts a tldraw drawing through the converter plugin-canvas registers:
    a drawing the scene DSL manages converts as every variant does, a hand-drawn one shape by shape. The copy is filed
    beside the tldraw drawing and opened.
  actors: human
  covers: []
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - plugin: Canvas and tldraw are enabled
    - space: a tldraw drawing holding a labelled rectangle, an ellipse, an arrow bound between them and a frame
  steps:
    - name: Copy it
      do: Open the tldraw drawing's object menu and choose "Copy as Canvas".
      expect: a canvas drawing of the same name opens, filed beside the tldraw one, which is unchanged
    - name: Read the copy
      do: Move the rectangle, then select a shape inside the frame.
      expect: the link follows the rectangle; the frame is a labelled rectangle under its shapes, which sit on the drawing itself and select like any other
```

```mdl
test QA-3: DXOS Architecture tour
  desc: |
    A walk through the DXOS Architecture template: the three reading guides and the nested drawings
    they embed, drilling from Composer's overview down to EDGE's replication layer. Recorded as a demo by
    autocue/architecture-tour.mjs.
  actors: human
  status: passing           # 2026-10-16, recorded by autocue/architecture-tour.mjs
  given:
    - an identity exists, on a fresh profile
    - plugin: Canvas is enabled in the plugin registry (the template is contributed only while it is)
    - space: a space created from the "DXOS Architecture" template, open, with its Architecture collection expanded
  steps:
    - name: Read the Composer guide
      do: Open the Composer document and scroll to its embedded drawing.
      expect: the Composer overview renders read-only inside the document
    - name: Open the Composer drawing
      do: Open the Composer drawing from the Architecture collection.
      expect: twelve boxes in three groups, five of them frames onto other diagrams
    - name: Drill into EDGE
      do: Open the EDGE frame, then db-service, then "Subduction repl.", each with its open control.
      expect: each opens the diagram beneath it, four levels deep, and the breadcrumb reads EDGE › db-service › Subduction
    - name: Come back up
      do: Click Up until the Composer overview is back.
      expect: the overview shows again
    - name: Read the EDGE guide
      do: Open the EDGE document.
      expect: the EDGE drawing is embedded, with a sequence diagram of a device connecting below it
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: Converting a tldraw drawing to a canvas always makes a copy. When the diagram language manages the drawing, the conversion uses the generic scene converter; otherwise it falls back to the hand-drawn importer, shape by shape.
  date: 2026-10-20
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [QA-5]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: plugin-canvas registers the hybrid conversion as an illustrator `Converter` for tldraw to canvas, which replaces the `ImportTldraw` operation; the copy is filed beside the source drawing and opened. Fills in [D-1].
  date: 2026-10-20
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [QA-5]
  why: The illustrator's Convert operation already resolves variants and files the result, so a per-pair converter reuses it rather than duplicating it.
  rejected:
    - A separate plugin-canvas import operation, which would offer a second, conflicting menu action.
```

```mdl
decision D-3: plugin-canvas builds on `org.dxos.plugin.illustrator` and `org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas`, and its spec references both rather than restating them.
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: user:richburdon
```

```mdl
decision D-4: The spec declares its dependencies rather than listing them in prose: `org.dxos.plugin.illustrator` in the `plugin` block's `dependsOn`, exactly as dx.config.ts declares it, and `org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas` under `## Dependencies`. Fills in [D-3].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: user:richburdon
```

```mdl
decision D-5: The `plugin CanvasPlugin` block lists the twelve modules plugin.tsx adds, one line each, and the `uses` reason keeps the prose list's wording. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  why: The plugin dialect requires `modules:` on every `plugin` block; reusing the prose keeps what the spec says about the engine unchanged.
```

```mdl
decision D-6: The plugin's detail page links to this spec: dx.config.ts names `PLUGIN.mdl` as the plugin's spec.
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
```

```mdl
decision D-7: The plugin serves this document with a `PluginAsset` module, listed in the `plugin` block, and the package exports `./assets/PLUGIN.mdl`, as other plugins with a spec do. Fills in [D-6].
  date: 2026-10-27
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: The registry's open-spec action appears only when a plugin's asset module supplies the spec named by its profile.
```

```mdl
decision D-8: Migrating a scene drawing turns the timelines its Views stored into one animator `Animation`, a shot per View with keyed tracks, staging that View's migrated scene: the same duration and keys, node tracks keying the element of the same id at its element path (`style.*` becomes `overrides.*`, `text` becomes `label`), camera tracks unchanged. The plugin registers `Animation` and `StyleCatalog` so the migration and its models can store them. Fills in COMMON-CORE decision 16.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  why: Canvas animation moved to the animator and Views stopped storing timelines, so a drawing saved before that keeps its animation only if the migration carries it over.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: The plugin contributes a Canvas and a 3D Canvas variant over the scene model's ECHO objects; a drawing's article and card render the shared scene editor with the 2D or 3D config its scene asks for, and a drawing saved before `Canvas` keeps a hidden legacy variant that renders the 2D engine on its own until its article migrates it. Fills in [D-3].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: The canvas engine is now the 2D viewport of a shared editor rather than the whole of a drawing's model, so the plugin builds on the editor, its two variants and the scene model.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: A Canvas's mode (lattice, constrained, dynamic) is each 2D layout's own field, which the shared editor applies; the canvas settings' `lattice` and `projection` are read only to move the mode onto the layouts, when a legacy drawing migrates or its article opens. `CanvasOperation.Apply` on a Canvas takes `LayoutIntent`s in its layout's dimension, recorded over every layout the canvas lists; the tldraw importer writes `Element2D`s through create intents. Fills in [D-9].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: A mode the settings named for every View cannot travel with a layout or differ between layouts, and agents, the importer and the editor then share one write path and one undo.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: Approve [D-10].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
```

```mdl
decision D-12: CanvasOperation.Apply on a Canvas snaps an agent's edits to its layout's mode, and an undo patch recorded before layouts held the mode is dropped rather than migrated.
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
```

```mdl
decision D-13: `CanvasOperation.Apply` runs the shared editor's 2D mode rule (`constrain2D`) with snapping on over the whole batch of intents, and fails with `InvalidIntentError`, writing nothing, when the mode refuses it; the rule lives in react-ui-scene's `canvas` entry, which the plugin's operations already load. Fills in [D-12].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: One rule keeps an agent's edit and a person's drag on the same cells, and checking the batch whole matches how the editor refuses a drag; the `canvas` entry keeps the editor and the 3D renderer out of the operation handlers.
  rejected:
    - Importing `constrain2D` from react-ui-scene's root, which would load the editor's React components and the 3D renderer into every operation handler.
    - Dropping only the refused intents, which would apply part of a batch the agent meant as one edit.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: A legacy scene canvas (a `Drawing.Canvas` in the `dxos.org/scene/2` format) is read only through the scene migration: its article migrates it onto a `Canvas` on open (`migrateSceneDrawing`), while its section, slide, card and animator stage draw `legacyDocument` read-only through the 2D scene view, and a canvas a newer plugin saved stays read-only under its banner. `CanvasOperation.Apply` and `AddGenerator` migrate a legacy drawing before writing, `InspectGenerator` reads it as the migration does without writing, and `CanvasOperation.Patch` refuses a legacy canvas. Fills in [D-9].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: The canvas engine's View model, intents and store are being retired (COMMON-CORE E.6), so one migration path must be the only reader of legacy records.
  rejected:
    - Keeping a writable legacy engine view, which would keep the View model and its store alive for one dialect.
    - Migrating on any surface, which would write a document a slide or card only looks at.
```

```mdl
decision D-15: `CanvasOperation.Apply` takes `constrain2D` from react-ui-canvas's root, which loads neither the editor nor the 3D renderer, and a 3D Canvas drawing shows the 3D variant `org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas.3d`. Fills in [D-13] and [org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas#D-37].
  date: 2026-10-30
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:richburdon
  affects: [CanvasPlugin]
  why: react-ui-scene, whose `canvas` entry held the rule, is removed; react-ui-canvas's root keeps the editor and Babylon out of the operation handlers, as that entry did, which its entries test checks.
  rejected:
    - Moving `constrain2D` into react-ui-canvas's `/headless` entry now, which needs the modes freed of the utilities barrel first.
```
